  "response_text": "PSP was set with the __set_PSP function so that the processor used a valid stack pointer to restore the state of the thread. If we did not do this, the CPU would try to access an invalid location in memory, which would cause the hard fault crash. The CPU uses the MSP by default, but threads should use the PSP to separate between system and thread stacks. Having this partition between the two stacks ensures that threads and stacks use their own respective memory space. Before running each thread, we should set the PSP to point to the thread's stack so that there is a valid location for the CPU to store register state - if not, the CPU could use an invalid stack location and hard fault.\\n",
